<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacques-René de Brisay de Denonville</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1637">1637</a>–<a href="/years/1710">1710</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Marquis de Denonville</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacques-René de Brisay, Marquis de Denonville, was governor general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> from 1685 to 1689. In 1687, after seizing Iroquois men at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-frontenac">Fort Frontenac</a> and sending some to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, he invaded the country of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> and burned their villages and crops. The campaign provoked Iroquois retaliation, including the raid on Lachine in 1689, and he was recalled to France the same year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacques-rene-de-brisay-de-denonville.jpg" alt="Jacques-René de Brisay de Denonville" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Rene_de_Brisay,_Marquis_de_Denonville.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born on 10 December 1637, Jacques-René de Brisay was the seventh child and eldest son of Pierre de Brisay and Louise d'Alès de Corbet, of the minor nobility; the family's seigneury of Denonville lay 64 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He entered the army young, served as a captain in the Régiment Royal in the North African campaign of 1664 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> pirates and later in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> with the Queen's Dragoons, and rose to brigadier in 1683. In November 1668 he married Catherine Courtin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> appointed him to replace Joseph-Antoine Le Fèbvre de La Barre, whose failed expedition of 1684 against the Haudenosaunee had ended in what the king called a shameful peace. Denonville reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> on 1 August 1685. He recruited Canadians into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troupes-de-la-marine">Troupes de la Marine</a>, founded a navigation school at Quebec, regulated taverns, restricted fur-trading licences and had a palisade built around Ville-Marie. In March 1686 he sent Pierre de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-le-moyne-diberville">Pierre Le Moyne d'Iberville</a> overland to James Bay, where they took Moose Factory, Rupert House and Fort Albany from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a>.</p><p>His campaign of 1687 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> brought together 832 regulars, over 900 Canadian militia and some 400 Indigenous allies. On the way, 51 men and 150 women and children of nearby villages were made prisoners at the fort at Cataraqui. Ambushed on 13 July, the French drove off the Seneca, and the army destroyed Ganondagan, Totiakton and two smaller villages with their stores of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> before building Fort Denonville on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niagara-river">Niagara River</a>. On the king's orders he reluctantly sent 36 male prisoners to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as galley slaves; only 13 survived to return in 1689. A peace he negotiated in June 1688 collapsed when war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> came, and on 5 August 1689 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> attacked Lachine, killing 24 inhabitants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The king recalled him to serve in his armies. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> he was promoted to major general and became deputy governor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s grandson Louis, then from 1690 to the Duke of Anjou and from 1693 to Charles, Duke of Berry. He died at his chateau of Denonville on 22 September 1710 and was buried in the crypt of its chapel. In 1937 the Rochester Numismatic Association struck a medal for the 250th anniversary of his expedition.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacques-René de Brisay, Marquis de Denonville” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Marquis de Tracy burned Mohawk villages in 1666, the <b>Marquis de Denonville</b> invaded Seneca country in 1687, and Frontenac struck the Onondaga in 1696, while Haudenosaunee warriors raided Lachine near Montreal in 1689.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1687 the French governor <b>Denonville</b> burned Ganondagan.</blockquote><a href="/state/seneca-nation">Seneca Nation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Denonville, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Denonville, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.39,1.81&amp;year=1684" aria-label="Denonville on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4592 472 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jacques-René de Brisay de Denonville"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5042 742h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5042 742h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.39,1.81&amp;year=1684">What was here in 1684? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
